# Fires the birthday bot each morning, then twice more as a safety net.
#
# WHY THREE FIRINGS. The bot gets one chance a day at something that cannot be
# done late — a greeting that shows up tomorrow is not a birthday greeting. A
# single blip at 09:00 (Slack having a bad minute, the venue's link flapping,
# MSSQL still starting up) used to mean the message simply never happened, and
# the only trace was a line in a log nobody reads.
#
# The extra firings cost nothing and cannot double-post: the bot records who it
# has greeted, so a run that finds the job already done exits without saying a
# word. If 09:00 worked, 09:30 and 10:30 are no-ops. If it didn't, they are the
# reason somebody still gets wished a happy birthday.
#
# Persistent=true covers the other half of the problem: a box that was powered
# off at 09:00 runs the job as soon as it boots.
#
# TIMEZONE — read this before changing the times.
#
# systemd fires OnCalendar on the SYSTEM timezone (`timedatectl`), which is NOT
# necessarily the app's (`--print-timezone`). Where they differ, a bare 09:00
# means 09:00 on the MACHINE's clock: at a venue running the host on UTC and
# the app on Eastern, this file posted the greeting at 05:00 local, and nothing
# said so — the timer looked right, the bot looked right, and the message
# landed before dawn.
#
# On systemd 252+ pin the zone onto each line so the time means what it says:
#
#     OnCalendar=*-*-* 09:00:00 America/New_York
#
# That is DST-safe, unlike converting to a fixed UTC offset. install.sh writes
# this form automatically when the two zones differ; the plain form below is
# the shipped default because it parses on every systemd version.
#
# After editing: `systemctl daemon-reload && systemctl restart ceplay-birthdays.timer`
# then confirm with `systemctl list-timers ceplay-birthdays`.

[Unit]
Description=CEplay birthday Slack bot — daily greeting

[Timer]
OnCalendar=*-*-* 09:00:00
OnCalendar=*-*-* 09:30:00
OnCalendar=*-*-* 10:30:00
Persistent=true
RandomizedDelaySec=0

[Install]
WantedBy=timers.target
